// A PARAMETER named after an emitter intrinsic. `vl check` returns 0 — the call is typed against
// the parameter's own `(i32) => i32` — and the emitter refuses with
// `emitProgram: __memory_size__ takes no arguments` (`compiler/wasmEmit.vl:15119`).
// Should print 2.
//
// MECHANISM. `checkNotEmitterIntrinsic` guards a FUNCTION name (`typecheck.vl:24827`) and a
// BINDING name (`typecheck.vl:35529`) — and not a parameter name. So the checker lets the name
// be bound, types the call against the binding, and accepts arity 1; `emitCall` then dispatches
// on `callee.identName` BEFORE any declaration lookup and lands in the intrinsic's arity-0 arm.
//
// EITHER RESOLUTION CLOSES IT: extend `checkNotEmitterIntrinsic` to parameters (then the
// diagnosis is the checker's, which it owes), or make the emitter's dispatch consult the scope
// before the name. What is not defensible is the state where a legal parameter name produces an
// emit-time complaint about an intrinsic the program never mentions.
//
// `__trap__` is NOT reachable this way — measured: its arity predicate is duplicated ungated in
// the checker (`typecheck.vl:23960`), so the shadowing spelling is refused at check.
function run(__memory_size__: (i32) => i32): i32 { return __memory_size__(1) }
print(
  run((n: i32) => {
    return n + 1
  }),
)
